ALFASKOP 91 + DU 4110 (Ericsson, 1988) - pack for @OS@
@ULINE@

What it is
----------
An Ericsson Alfaskop System 91 terminal controller (68000, pSOS) with a
DU 4110 display unit and its keyboard, emulated in MAME (@EXE@, a build of
MAME 0.288 with the A91 driver, inside the pack@LIBS@: nothing needs to be
installed@DESKTOP@).

The diskettes are the three that Mattis Lind read from his own A91:
  A91R4A_1.IMD  A91 system, version V5.6-4A, 10 June 1988
  A91R4A_2.IMD  emulation "ALFASKOP 91 Single Line IBM 3270 SNA switch 5.6H"
  A91SETUP.IMD  another system diskette, version M5.06-02 (1988)

The A91 is a 3270 controller that connects over switched X.21 (the Datex
network) to an IBM mainframe running SNA. There is no mainframe here: the
emulator carries a minimal "host" that calls the A91 over X.21, brings up the
SDLC link and the SNA session, and sends it a 3270 screen that echoes what is
typed. Everything else (the A91, the DU, the keyboard, the diskettes) is the
original software, untouched.

With START-A91-MAINFRAME the A91 connects instead to a real emulated IBM
mainframe, and the DU logs on to TSO: see "With a real IBM mainframe".


How to start it (step by step)
------------------------------
1. @STEP1@
   A black window opens (not full screen): it is the screen of the DU.
   Below it there is a grey strip with two red digits: the display of the
   A91's front panel (see "The A91 panel" below).

2. From 0 to about 50 seconds: "!LOAD" at the bottom left.
   The A91 is reading the diskettes and starting. Nothing needs to be done.
   Meanwhile the panel digits count through the boot and then blink.

3. At about 55 seconds: "!*OS*" at the bottom.
   The DU has received its program over the A91's bus.

4. At about 65 seconds, in the middle of the screen:
       ********** A U T O L O G O N **********
   The DU introduces itself. Still nothing to do.

5. At about 85 seconds the host screen appears:
       ALFASKOP 91 HOST - SNA OVER X.21 - EMULATED HOST AT DIAL NUMBER 100852
       SESSION BOUND. THE A91 ANSWERED ACTPU, ACTLU, BIND AND SDT.
       TYPE HERE:
   and at the bottom the status line:  !EMA01A MY JOB      PR04      SNSW 007/013
   It is now in a 3270 session.

6. Type something after TYPE HERE: and press Enter.
   The host answers on the same screen:
       LAST KEY: ENTER
       YOU TYPED: (what was typed)
   With F1 to F12 it answers LAST KEY: PF1 ... PF12, and so on for the rest.

The times are at normal speed; a slow PC takes somewhat longer.


With a real IBM mainframe (TSO on MVS)
--------------------------------------
START-A91-MAINFRAME@EXT@ connects the A91, instead of to the minimal host, to a
whole emulated IBM mainframe:
  - an IBM 3705 communications controller running IBM's NCP network program
    (emulator by Edwin Freekenhorst and Henk Stegeman);
  - a mainframe with MVS 3.8j, VTAM and TSO (TK5, by Rob Prins) on Hercules.
The A91 calls, the NCP activates it, VTAM sends it its logon screen, and from
the DU 4110 you log on to TSO with a user and password, as in 1988.
@REQ@
Everything is inside the pack (folder mainframe@SEP@). The 3705 and MVS have no
window: their messages go to mainframe@SEP@log.

Step by step:

1. @STEP1MF@
   A text window opens and reports what is happening. Nothing needs to be
   typed in it.
     "Starting the 3705 and MVS, about 2 minutes."   MVS IPL: about 45 s
     "MVS is up. Loading the NCP into the 3705"       NCP load: 1 minute
     "NCP loaded. Opening the Alfaskop 91."
   After 2 minutes the A91 window opens, as in the normal mode.

2. The A91 starts as always: !LOAD, !*OS*, AUTOLOGON (about 65 s).
   About a minute into the A91 window, the text window says
     L16A20 active / P16A20A active / T16A20A1 active
   and on the DU the status line changes to  !EMA01A SYS OP
   That is the VTAM logon screen. At the top it says once or twice
     UNSUPPORTED FUNCTION
   which is not an error (see below).

3. Type:  LOGON APPLID(TSO) LOGMODE(MHP3278E)   and press Enter.
   The status line changes to  MY JOB  and a few seconds later an X and the
   number 443 appear:  MY JOB    X       443   with an empty screen.
   TSO has asked the DU what it can do, and the DU does not answer that
   question. Nothing is wrong.

4. Press left Ctrl (RESET): the X goes away. Press F3 (PF3).
   Between 30 s and 1 minute later
     IKJ56700A ENTER USERID -
   appears. If an X shows again in the meantime (443 or C01), RESET and F3 again.

5. Type  HERC01  and Enter.  ENTER CURRENT PASSWORD FOR HERC01-  appears.
   Type  CUL8TR  and Enter.

6. 5 to 30 s later: HERC01 LOGON IN PROGRESS, the box
     Welcome to the TSO system on TK5R
   and  ***  at the bottom. Press Enter.

7. A drawing of a cat and a sentence (the "fortune cookie") appear with ***.
   Here press Page Up (PA1), NOT Enter: Enter would open TK5's ISPF menu,
   which is in colour, and the DU 4110 has no colours (see below).
   ! *  and  READY  appear.

8. You are in TSO. For example:
     LISTCAT        lists the files of user HERC01
     TIME           (answers only READY on this system)
     LOGOFF         ends the session; the VTAM screen comes back (step 3)

9. To finish, close the A91 window. The text window shuts down MVS and the
   3705 by itself (about 30 s) and closes. If the text window was closed
   first, STOP@EXT@ closes everything of this pack.

What may look alarming in this mode and is not an error:
- UNSUPPORTED FUNCTION on the VTAM screen: the A91 tells VTAM that the DU has
  loaded (NOTIFY), and this version of VTAM does not know that message.
- X 443 right after typing LOGON: it is the TSO question the DU does not
  answer. RESET and F3.
- UNOWNED for a moment after LOGON: the program that takes the logons hands
  the session to the user's TSO. If X C01 shows afterwards, RESET and F3.
- If Enter is pressed at the fortune cookie: X 470 and the message
  IKT00405I SCREEN ERASURE CAUSED BY ERROR RECOVERY PROCEDURE again and
  again. It is the ISPF menu in colour. Press Page Up (PA1) and READY comes.
- The sides of the welcome box show as $: the DU uses the Swedish character
  set and that code is another letter in TK5's.
- If the text window says something "did not become active", close the A91
  window and start again: every start does a fresh IPL of MVS.


The keyboard
------------
The DU has a Swedish Ericsson keyboard. The PC keys map by position:

  Letters and digits       in their places
  Shift + 1 2 3 4 ...      ! " # $ % & / ( ) =   (4 gives the currency sign,
                                                  which this screen draws as $)
  key right of 0           + ?
  the next one             e with accent (é É)
  key right of P           å Å
  the next one             ü ^
  key right of L           ö Ö
  the next one             ä Ä
  key next to Enter        ' *
  key left of Z            < >
  , . and the one right    , ;   . :   - _

  Enter                    ENTER (sends the screen to the host)
  keypad Enter             NEW LINE (to the first field of the next line)
  Tab                      next field
  Arrows                   move the cursor
  Backspace                cursor left (as on a 3270)
  Home                     cursor to the start
  Delete                   deletes the character
  Insert                   insert mode
  End                      CLEAR (clears the screen and tells the host)
  left Ctrl                RESET (unlocks the keyboard when an X shows)
  F1 to F12                PF1 to PF12
  Shift + F1 to F12        PF13 to PF24
  Page Up / Page Down      PA1 / PA2  (PA3 has no key on the PC)
  Print Screen             print (see below)
  Caps Lock                shift lock (released with Shift, as on a
                           typewriter)
  left Alt                 Alt of the Ericsson keyboard
  Alt + `                  DEV CNCL (` is the key at the top left, under Esc)

The PC keys go straight to the DU. To reach MAME's menu (for example to
change a key): Scroll Lock and then Tab. Scroll Lock again to go back to the
DU keyboard.


The A91 panel
-------------
The two red digits under the screen are the display of the controller's front
panel. In a normal boot it shows:
  03 04 05             ROM self test, the first second
  06 07 ... 20 30 00   system load from the diskette, up to 20 s
  60 61 50 51          boot status codes, one after another, each one second
                       on and one off, from 20 to 50 s
  00                   everything running
The panel drawing is deduced from the A91's code, which sends two decimal
digits; it is not copied from photographs of the unit.


What may look alarming and is not an error
------------------------------------------
- The panel digits blinking between 20 and 50 seconds (60, 61, 50, 51): they
  are boot status codes, not errors. At the end it stays at 00.
- The "!" at the start of the status line is always there: it is a normal
  DU indicator.
- "!LOAD" for almost a minute: it is the load from diskette.
- Print Screen: as there is no printer, "X   PR FAILURE" appears on the status
  line and the keyboard locks. Alt + ` (DEV CNCL) clears it.
- Alt + Print Screen asks "ASSIGN A PR  04": it is the printer assignment.
  Enter keeps the one there was (04) and everything returns to normal.
- The currency sign shows as $: the character set of this DU 4110 has $ at
  that code.


The SETUP diskette
------------------
START-SETUP-DISK@EXT@ starts the A91 with the A91SETUP diskette. It reaches a
screen "Alfaskop 91 Customer Setup Test / Test that the keyboard works" with
a manual load line at the bottom:  MAN V:  L:  M/F:  M:
That diskette carries its configuration program for the Alfaskop 9014 (VGA)
display, not for the DU 4110: with the 4110 you can only check that the
keyboard types on that line. If you try to load something (for example the
library D7GCSU), the DU hangs: close the window and start again.


How to close it
---------------
Close the window with the X, at any time. The A91 has no shutdown command and
no open file system that could be damaged, and a normal session writes
nothing to the diskettes (checked: they stay identical).

STOP@EXT@ is only needed if the window hangs.

The diskettes in use are in the folder disks@SEP@. The originals are in
disks@SEP@originals and are never touched. RESET-DISKS@EXT@ closes the emulator
and copies the originals over the working ones again.


The files
---------
  START-A91@EXT@            the A91 with the R4A diskettes and the host
  START-SETUP-DISK@EXT@     the A91 with the SETUP diskette
  START-A91-MAINFRAME@EXT@  the A91 connected to the IBM mainframe (3705 + MVS)
  mainframe@SEP@                the 3705, Hercules and MVS (TK5); mainframe@SEP@log their messages
  RESET-DISKS@EXT@          restores the working diskettes
  STOP@EXT@                 closes the emulator if it hung
  @EXE@@EXEPAD@                MAME 0.288 with the A91 driver
@LIBLINE@  roms@SEP@                    ROMs of the A91, the DU 4110 and the keyboard
  disks@SEP@originals@SEP@         the three diskettes as they came
  snapshots@SEP@                snapshots go here: Scroll Lock, F12, Scroll Lock

Mattis Lind read the ROMs and the diskettes from his own unit; the sources of
everything in this pack are at https://github.com/ajfa/alfaskop-91
